 UNPCK:,NAME,
C===========================================================
C unpck(value, p) - unpack one word into six characters.
C
C Calling convention for a two-argument ASSEMBLER routine:
C   ACC    = p, the word address of six characters
C   [SP-1] = value, the word the characters come from
C   M13    = return link
C
C   void unpck(int, int *) assembler;
C
C Result:
C   p[0]..p[5] hold the six eight-bit characters of value, the
C   most significant byte of it in p[0].
C
C P/UN wants the destination address in M12 and the word in the
C accumulator, and that is the whole of what it wants.  An
C unpacked character array gives every element a word of its
C own, which is why the destination is an INT * and not a
C CHAR *, a CHAR * being a byte index into flat memory.
C
C The word is the pushed argument and the address arrives in the
C accumulator, so STI is the whole of the entry sequence: it
C takes the address for M12 and pops the word into the
C accumulator in one instruction.  That is what makes this order
C of the two arguments the convenient one.
C
C P/UN returns through M13, which still holds the caller's link,
C so the jump into it is the return as well.
C===========================================================
 P/UN:          ,SUBP,
                ,NTR ,7      . ABI requirement
                ,STI ,12     . M12 := p; ACC := value
                ,UJ  ,P/UN   . tail call: P/UN returns to our caller
                ,END ,
